Q3: This exercise gives an illustration for polymorphism at behavior level.
Recall that for each type of media, we have implemented a toString() method that prints out the
information of the object. When calling this method, depending on the type of object, corresponding
toString() will be performed. Create an ArrayList of Media, then add some media (CD, DVD or Book) into the list. 
Iterate through the list and print out the information of the media by using toString() method. Observe
what happens and explain in detail.
- As I override the toString() method of each subclass of the Media class, the output of the method toString() will be different for each class despite being in the same loop. This is due to dynamic binding in Java being implemented implicitly. To be more specific, when media points to a Book object, Java will execute the toString() method for Book and the same happens to other classes as well.

Q4: Alternatively, to compare items in the cart, instead of using Comparator, we can use the
Comparable interface and override the compareTo() method. You can refer to the Java docs to see the
information of this interface.
Suppose we are taking this Comparable interface approach.
- What class should implement the Comparable interface?
- In those classes, how should you implement the compareTo() method be to reflect the ordering that we
want?
- Can we have two ordering rules of the item (by title then cost and by cost then title) if we use this
Comparable interface approach?
- Suppose the DVDs has a different ordering rule from the other media types, that is by title, then decreasing
length, then cost. How would you modify your code to allow this?

- The class that should implement the Comparable interface is the Media class.
- As we need to sort Media objects in Cart2 and Store, we need to implement in the parent class to ensure that every kind of class that inherits from the Media class can be compared to each other.
- To reflect the ordering that we want, we should implement the compareTo() method in the Media class, sorting by title then cost if the titles are identical.
- We cannot have 2 ordering rules if we use comparable. This is the main limitation of the Comparable interface as a class only has one compareTo() method. Therefore, the AIMS system needs to implement the Comparator interface instead of Comparable.
- If a DVD has a different ordering rule, I will override the method in the DigitalVideoDisc2 class. After checking the titles, if the titles are different, I will check whether the object passed into the method is a DigitalVideoDisc2 or not. If it is a DigitalVideoDisc2, I will compare the length of the two objects. Else, I will jump to the float cost comparison. The code is illustrated below.

	public int compareTo(Media other) {
        int titleComparison = this.getTitle().compareToIgnoreCase(other.getTitle());
        
        if (titleComparison != 0) {
            return titleComparison;
        }
        
        if (other instanceof DigitalVideoDisc2) {
            DigitalVideoDisc2 otherDVD = (DigitalVideoDisc2) other;
            
            int lengthComparison = Integer.compare(otherDVD.getLength(), this.getLength());
            
            if (lengthComparison != 0) {
                return lengthComparison; 
            }
        }
        
        return Float.compare(other.getCost(), this.getCost());
    }

